1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,865
In this video, we're going
to be talking about using

2
00:00:02,865 --> 00:00:05,595
time with your user
space programs.

3
00:00:05,595 --> 00:00:08,040
We're going to talk about
different time measurements

4
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:09,960
and formats that are
supported by Linux,

5
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:10,620
we're going to talk about

6
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:12,180
different clock
types you can use

7
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:14,950
for obtaining time
and setting time,

8
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:17,310
and then we're going to talk
about the functions you can

9
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:20,280
use for getting and setting
time from user space.

10
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:22,560
There's three different
measurement types

11
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:24,060
that the book discusses.

12
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:27,645
There's wall time, process
time, and monotonic time.

13
00:00:27,645 --> 00:00:29,940
The wall time is the actual time

14
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:31,380
and date to real-world clocks,

15
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:32,550
so what you see in the clock on

16
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:33,915
your system, for instance.

17
00:00:33,915 --> 00:00:36,670
That one's often useful
for user interaction,

18
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:38,015
so when you're
talking to the user

19
00:00:38,015 --> 00:00:39,680
about some specific event,

20
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:40,925
the time that it happened,

21
00:00:40,925 --> 00:00:43,085
when you're giving the time now,

22
00:00:43,085 --> 00:00:45,895
this is the time measurement
that you'd want to use.

23
00:00:45,895 --> 00:00:47,700
It's going to be
localized so that

24
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:50,255
means time zone information
is going to be relevant.

25
00:00:50,255 --> 00:00:52,025
That means daylight
savings time,

26
00:00:52,025 --> 00:00:53,900
for instance, it's
going to be relevant.

27
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:57,170
It might be saved
and restored using

28
00:00:57,170 --> 00:01:02,760
battery-powered hardware and
special hardware support

29
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:04,725
so a real-time
clock for instance.

30
00:01:04,725 --> 00:01:08,240
It's often updated with
Network Time Protocol or NTP,

31
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:11,000
which allows us to synchronize
with clocks that are

32
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,060
available and known
good references times.

33
00:01:15,710 --> 00:01:17,870
The other types of

34
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:20,000
time measurements that
we can get from Linux

35
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,750
besides wall time
is process time.

36
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:25,040
This would be the time that's

37
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:27,575
spent executing on a processor.

38
00:01:27,575 --> 00:01:30,125
The total time that

39
00:01:30,125 --> 00:01:32,330
your process spends
executing on a processor,

40
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:33,995
could be less than wall time

41
00:01:33,995 --> 00:01:36,650
because your process is
going to get interrupted.

42
00:01:36,650 --> 00:01:37,880
Or it actually could be more

43
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:39,650
than wall time
because if you have

44
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:41,720
multiprocessors and
you're splitting

45
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:43,940
your process across
multiple processors,

46
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:45,920
the total amount of
process time could

47
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:49,045
actually be more than the
amount of wall clock time.

48
00:01:49,045 --> 00:01:52,980
Then monotonic time which
is always increasing.

49
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:56,360
You can think of that
as system up time,

50
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:59,310
the amount of time that the
system has been running.

51
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:03,290
The time measurement
formats that we'll use with

52
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:07,145
these different
time measurements

53
00:02:07,145 --> 00:02:10,300
are relative time
or absolute time.

54
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:12,740
Relative time is going
to be relative to

55
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:14,420
some specific time so now

56
00:02:14,420 --> 00:02:16,565
would be an example
of a relative time.

57
00:02:16,565 --> 00:02:19,940
Then you can have
a relationship to

58
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:21,650
the current time being something

59
00:02:21,650 --> 00:02:24,070
like five seconds from
now, 10 minutes ago.

60
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:26,900
For this you would typically
want to use monotonic time

61
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:29,210
because wall clock time

62
00:02:29,210 --> 00:02:31,460
we could be adjusting
that, for instance,

63
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:35,645
due to NTP or due to an
adjustment from the user,

64
00:02:35,645 --> 00:02:37,890
or due to something like
daylight savings time,

65
00:02:37,890 --> 00:02:40,250
and we wouldn't want
our relative time to

66
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:44,200
change based on changes
made to a specific clock.

67
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,510
Then absolute time measurements
aren't relative to

68
00:02:47,510 --> 00:02:48,845
a benchmark they describe

69
00:02:48,845 --> 00:02:51,245
a certain point in
wall clock time.

70
00:02:51,245 --> 00:02:53,465
September 24th at 3:00 PM,

71
00:02:53,465 --> 00:02:54,560
Mountain Daylight Time would be

72
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:56,165
an example of an absolute time.

73
00:02:56,165 --> 00:02:58,240
It's something that you
would see in a calendar.

74
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:01,115
In Linux these are all tract
relative to the epoch.

75
00:03:01,115 --> 00:03:05,040
The epoch was midnight,
January 1st, 1970.

76
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:07,475
When they're writing
the POSIX library,

77
00:03:07,475 --> 00:03:10,220
they figured no computer
was going to need to deal

78
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:13,040
with a current clock time
earlier than January 1st,

79
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:15,665
1970 based on when the
standard was written,

80
00:03:15,665 --> 00:03:19,520
so they use this as the
time zero essentially for

81
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:24,870
calculating when an absolute
time event is occurring.

82
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:27,275
How does this actually

83
00:03:27,275 --> 00:03:29,330
get calculated by
the Linux kernel?

84
00:03:29,330 --> 00:03:31,010
Turns out there's a system timer

85
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:32,570
that we'll talk in
more detail about

86
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:35,870
when we get into the specifics
of kernel programming,

87
00:03:35,870 --> 00:03:38,100
and it's using a specific
interval frequency.

88
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:40,700
It's going to increment the
elapsed time by one unit

89
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:43,310
every time there's an
interval that ends,

90
00:03:43,310 --> 00:03:44,960
and those intervals
are referred to

91
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:46,915
in the kernel as
ticks or jiffies.

92
00:03:46,915 --> 00:03:48,810
A jiffy counter in the kernel is

93
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:50,660
going to be a 64-bit value

94
00:03:50,660 --> 00:03:52,610
that is incremented

95
00:03:52,610 --> 00:03:55,640
every hertz frequency
of the system timer.

96
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:57,890
The hertz value is
going to be platform

97
00:03:57,890 --> 00:04:02,930
independent but
historical x86 values

98
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:05,150
which is probably what you're
using if you're using a PC,

99
00:04:05,150 --> 00:04:06,770
for instance, would range

100
00:04:06,770 --> 00:04:08,840
from 100 hertz to 1,000 hertz so

101
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:10,340
that's typically the interval

102
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:13,480
that the software clock
is going to be updated.

103
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:16,455
What about jiffies
time rollover?

104
00:04:16,455 --> 00:04:19,395
If you have a 64-bit
jiffies timer,

105
00:04:19,395 --> 00:04:24,320
and you're using x86 and
the Jiffy is 0.004 seconds,

106
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:27,200
then the rollover would be
at about 5.8 billion years,

107
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,739
so nothing that we need to
worry about in our lifetimes.

108
00:04:30,739 --> 00:04:34,520
The frequency of the system
timer is called hertz,

109
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:37,805
and that's architecture
specific as mentioned before.

110
00:04:37,805 --> 00:04:39,470
The POSIX time APIs are

111
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:40,880
going to convert it
for us so when we're

112
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:44,045
in user space we don't care
about the value of hertz.

113
00:04:44,045 --> 00:04:45,770
We'll talk more about
that when we get in

114
00:04:45,770 --> 00:04:48,390
the kernel driver development.

115
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:53,690
The simplest time representation
in Linux is the time_t,

116
00:04:53,690 --> 00:04:56,225
which is defined as
typedef long time_t.

117
00:04:56,225 --> 00:04:58,220
This is going to
represent the number

118
00:04:58,220 --> 00:04:59,480
of seconds since the epoch

119
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:03,265
which was that January
1st, 1970 time.

120
00:05:03,265 --> 00:05:05,330
One thing to note about this,

121
00:05:05,330 --> 00:05:07,820
we talked about 64-bit
jiffies rollover,

122
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:10,790
but if we're counting
seconds since the epoch,

123
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:13,925
it turns out if we only
have a 32-bit long value,

124
00:05:13,925 --> 00:05:15,875
then we're going to roll over

125
00:05:15,875 --> 00:05:20,120
that 32 value on Monday the
18th of January in 2038.

126
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:23,180
If you remember reading
about the Y2K problem,

127
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:25,160
or if you're like me and
you live through it,

128
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:27,350
we have another one of
those coming up for

129
00:05:27,350 --> 00:05:30,740
32-bit systems in
January of 2038.

130
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:32,900
Hopefully most of
the 32-bit systems

131
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:34,745
will be phased out by that time.

132
00:05:34,745 --> 00:05:36,895
But no doubt,

133
00:05:36,895 --> 00:05:39,200
some group of us
is probably going

134
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:42,010
to be dealing with
this problem in 2038.

135
00:05:42,010 --> 00:05:44,090
One thing that you might ask

136
00:05:44,090 --> 00:05:45,770
is if we're only counting
seconds with this,

137
00:05:45,770 --> 00:05:48,545
how do I get sub-second
precision with timing?

138
00:05:48,545 --> 00:05:51,320
There's a solution for that that

139
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:54,595
we'll mention in a future slide.

140
00:05:54,595 --> 00:05:57,020
The clock real-time is one of

141
00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:58,220
the clock types
that's going to be

142
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:00,080
the system wide wall clock time.

143
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,630
That's the one you can
actually set or update based

144
00:06:02,630 --> 00:06:06,385
on information about the
correct current time.

145
00:06:06,385 --> 00:06:07,680
Then clock monotonic is

146
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:09,020
the one that's not
settable it's going

147
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:12,035
to be elapsed time since the
starting point like boot.

148
00:06:12,035 --> 00:06:13,700
There are other clocks that

149
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:15,200
your book covers as
well but these are

150
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:16,580
the main two that
we're going to discuss

151
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:18,640
for the purpose of this course.

152
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:22,295
When would you use clock
monotonic over clock real-time?

153
00:06:22,295 --> 00:06:23,540
You wouldn't want to use clock

154
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:24,620
real-time when you're measuring

155
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:27,320
intervals that are depending
on system time not changing.

156
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:30,170
Remember, if clock real-time can

157
00:06:30,170 --> 00:06:33,080
change and you're using a
measurement in relative time,

158
00:06:33,080 --> 00:06:35,540
you wouldn't want daylight
savings event, for instance,

159
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:36,860
to make you think
that you waited

160
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:39,085
an hour when you
actually didn't.

161
00:06:39,085 --> 00:06:42,905
The system might be
setting that time

162
00:06:42,905 --> 00:06:45,130
ahead or backwards depending

163
00:06:45,130 --> 00:06:48,625
on the time correction that
you're trying to perform.

164
00:06:48,625 --> 00:06:52,450
How do you get more
accurate representations

165
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:55,515
then no single digits seconds?

166
00:06:55,515 --> 00:06:57,970
You can use timeval
or timespec to get

167
00:06:57,970 --> 00:07:00,490
microsecond or
nanosecond resolution.

168
00:07:00,490 --> 00:07:01,510
There's a set of

169
00:07:01,510 --> 00:07:03,340
functions that you can use
with each one of these

170
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:08,255
to obtain or to set based
on higher precisions.

171
00:07:08,255 --> 00:07:10,545
There's also a TM,

172
00:07:10,545 --> 00:07:12,205
a representation which is

173
00:07:12,205 --> 00:07:13,810
going to give you the
human-readable time.

174
00:07:13,810 --> 00:07:16,374
This will give you
seconds, minutes, hours,

175
00:07:16,374 --> 00:07:18,970
the absolute time
that we referenced

176
00:07:18,970 --> 00:07:21,740
when we talked about an
example of absolute time.

177
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:23,530
There's a whole list of

178
00:07:23,530 --> 00:07:25,630
functions here that
you can use with this,

179
00:07:25,630 --> 00:07:27,499
and they're some suggestions

180
00:07:27,499 --> 00:07:28,610
about which of these you should

181
00:07:28,610 --> 00:07:31,700
use in terms of
being thread-safe,

182
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:34,460
for instance, or being
generally safer to

183
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:38,790
use for obtaining the time
representation as TM.

184
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:41,480
Talking specifically
about how we can

185
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:43,190
get clock time using

186
00:07:43,190 --> 00:07:44,720
the timespec structure which is

187
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:47,945
the higher precision that
we mentioned before.

188
00:07:47,945 --> 00:07:51,110
The clock time or

189
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:53,135
the get clock time function

190
00:07:53,135 --> 00:07:54,960
is going to take
o'clock Id argument.

191
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:55,970
That's going to be
one of the clocks

192
00:07:55,970 --> 00:07:57,050
that we mentioned before so

193
00:07:57,050 --> 00:08:00,170
real-time and monotonic
are two examples.

194
00:08:00,170 --> 00:08:03,420
Then the timespec structure
that needs to fill in.

195
00:08:04,490 --> 00:08:07,805
You'd want to use
clock real-time with

196
00:08:07,805 --> 00:08:09,620
clock set time when

197
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:11,190
you're actually using
it to set the time.

198
00:08:11,190 --> 00:08:12,320
Again, because clock

199
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:14,260
monotonic isn't going
to be settable.

200
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:16,670
It's important to note
that you might need

201
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:19,700
elevated permissions
to set the clock time,

202
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:21,230
you wouldn't want
unprivileged user

203
00:08:21,230 --> 00:08:23,760
to be able to
arbitrarily served.

204
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:27,605
There's also a add

205
00:08:27,605 --> 00:08:30,560
adjust time function
that can be used by

206
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:33,080
the NTP daemon that

207
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:37,570
begins slowly adjusting
the system time.

208
00:08:37,570 --> 00:08:40,010
The benefit of this one
is that it wouldn't

209
00:08:40,010 --> 00:08:41,660
move the clock backwards
which could be

210
00:08:41,660 --> 00:08:43,340
disruptive to programs that

211
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:45,350
are referencing absolute time.

212
00:08:45,350 --> 00:08:49,110
This is another option
for setting the time.